    {
      "ref": 2,
      "quote": "A 30-minute water preload doesn’t seem to work as well in younger compared to older individuals, which may be down to differences in the rate at which water leaves your stomach."
    },
    {
      "ref": 2,
      "quote": "As you get older, gastric emptying - which refers to the rate at which food and fluid leaves your stomach - tends to slow down."
    },
    {
      "ref": 2,
      "quote": "If you’re in your 20’s or 30’s, a water preload ingested 30 minutes before a meal will be almost completely emptied from your stomach by the time of the meal. As a result, it’s unlikely to influence your calorie intake."
    },
    {
      "ref": 2,
      "quote": "As such, anyone in their 20’s and 30’s will be better off having a water preload immediately before a meal, while someone in their 40’s, 50’s or beyond can expect to see a benefit with water consumed anywhere between 30 minutes and immediately prior to a meal."
    }
  ],
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "Gutless says the timing of a water preload should depend on your age because the rate at which water leaves your stomach differs with age. For younger individuals, a 30-minute water preload is less effective, and they are better off having a preload immediately before a meal. For older individuals, a water preload consumed anywhere between 30 minutes and immediately prior to a meal is more beneficial [2].",
  "clarification": ""
}

